  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure edging toward the area through the balance of the\nwork week brings drier air that will suppress early morning fog\nand preclude afternoon thunderstorms. A mid/upper-level short \nwave trough may be able to squeeze out a shower over the\nsouthern mountains Friday afternoon. The NBM suggestion of \nthunderstorms there then has no meteorological basis due to an \ninversion and therefore stable conditions, and the Storm \nPrediction Center keeps general thunderstorms south of the \nforecast area in their Day 2 Friday/Friday night outlook.\n\nAfter lows in the 50s throughout much of the area this morning,\nand highs in the 70s to low 80s this afternoon, temperatures\nslowly moderate, with lows in the 50s to low 60s Friday\nmorning, and highs in the mid 70s to mid 80s Friday afternoon.\nDew points in the 50s, 60s south, this afternoon will come up to\nthe 60s throughout the lowlands for Friday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAmid persistent long wave ridging in the west and troughing in\nthe east, short wave troughs cross each evening Friday and\nSaturday, from which showers could arise in and near the \nmountains. There may even be another short wave in there\nSaturday morning, and then at least a mid/upper-level jetlet \nSunday. By late Saturday afternoon, there may be enough \ninstability for a thunderstorm in the southern mountains, and\nfor Sunday afternoon, most anywhere there is precipitation, \nwhich the northern tier of the forecast area, as well as in and \nnear the mountains.\n\nTemperatures continue their slow moderation this weekend, \nclimbing to or a bit above normal, with dew points in the 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nLong wave ridging in the west and troughing in the east persists\nnext week. For the beginning of the next work week, a short \nwave trough and surface low pressure system traverse beneath\nthe northwest mid/upper-level flow. Low level southwest flow \nahead of the low and associated cold front beneath the mid/upper\nnorthwest flow may result in one or more convective complexes \nsailing southeastward across the area. The Weather Prediction \nCenter has introduced a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall for\ntheir new Day 5 Excessive Rainfall Outlook, as referenced in \ntheir latest Extended Forecast Discussion.\n\nWhile there has been some question as to how long this pattern\npersists next week before the cold front comes through and \nclears everything out, latest guidance suggests the cold front \ncoming to the rescue by midweek, and central guidance is \ntrending in that direction as well.\n\nGuidance has come into better focus with temperature next week,\njust above normal on Monday before settling to or a bit below \nnormal. Temperatures early next week may be modulated by the \ntiming and track of convective complexes.",
